<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melbourne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melbourne is the capital of Victoria and the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, on Port Phillip Bay at the mouth of the Yarra River. Founded in 1835 by settlers from Van Diemen's Land, it boomed after the gold rushes of the 1850s to become one of the largest cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. It served as the seat of the federal government from 1901 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> replaced it in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melbourne.jpg" alt="Melbourne" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Melbpal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_skyline_sor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parliament sat in <b>Melbourne</b> until 1927, when it moved to Canberra, a new capital built on federal territory between Sydney and Melbourne as a compromise between the two rival cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first exhibition outside China was held at the National Gallery of Victoria in <b>Melbourne</b> in 1982.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Botany Bay&quot; can be traced back to Little Jack Sheppard, a musical burlesque staged at the Gaiety Theatre in London in 1885 and in <b>Melbourne</b> in 1886.</blockquote><a href="/songs/botany-bay">Botany Bay</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1876 The Saturday Advertiser held a competition to set it to music, judged by three prominent <b>Melbourne</b> musicians, with a prize of ten guineas.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a></li><li><blockquote>That version was used for 47 years, until Addie MS recorded a new one in <b>Melbourne</b> in 1998 with his Twilite Orchestra, the Victorian Philharmonic Orchestra and the Melbourne Symphony Orchestra.</blockquote><a href="/songs/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers sent by John Pascoe Fawkner went ashore on the Yarra River, building the first houses of what became <b>Melbourne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
